[[:db/cas 17592186416358 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2-0)^{2}+(6-0)^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{11}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2-0)^{2}+(6-0)^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416359 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416359 :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$  \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$  \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{4\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [:db/cas 17592186416366 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416374 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4} & \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416376 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416382 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{4} & -\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416384 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416385 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{7} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{7} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{4}{7}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416386 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416388 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & -\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{4} & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & -\\frac{7}{4}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416389 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{4} & \\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & -\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{7}{4} & \\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & -\\frac{7}{4}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416390 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416396 :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & b & 4 \\\\ a & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & b & 4 \\\\ a & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,T({b}_{3})=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416396 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-7,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-7)+2=-4.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-7,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-7)+2=-4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416397 :question/problem "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & b & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}x+y+z \\\\ x+z \\\\ y-z\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & b & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}x+y+z \\\\ x+z \\\\ y-z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416397 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & -3 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-5,$ $b=3,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+3+2=0.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & -3 & -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -5 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{4}{3} & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-5,$ $b=3,$ $c=2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+3+2=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416398 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}.$ The matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & b & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+3{b}_{3}, \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & ={b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}, \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =2{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}+3{b}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}.$ The matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & b & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & c\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+3{b}_{3}, \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & ={b}_{1}+3{b}_{2}, \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =2{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2}+3{b}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416398 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the coordinates of the images relative to the basis $B:$\n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{3})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $b=3,$ $c=3$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=2+3+3=8.\n$$" "First, we write down the coordinates of the images relative to the basis $B:$\n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,[T({b}_{3})]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, the $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $b=3,$ $c=3$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=2+3+3=8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416399 :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}6x-7y \\\\ -2x-6y\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B={[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.$" "Let $B$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$, given that\n\n$\\,$ $T(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}6x-7y \\\\ -2x-6y\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B={\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186416399 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}50 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 & 50 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 & -20\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 & -20\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{10}{3} & \\frac{50}{3} & -\\frac{50}{3}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{10}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}50 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1})$ and $T({b}_{2})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 50 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 & -20\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 & -20\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{10}{3} & \\frac{50}{3} & -\\frac{50}{3}\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{10}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{25}{3} & \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416406 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1+6y-9z \\\\ y \\\\ 2-z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}1+6y-9z \\\\ y \\\\ 2-z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186416407 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416410 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-6y+9z \\\\ 4-y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}1-6y+9z \\\\ 4-y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186416412 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416414 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-6y+9z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}1-6y+9z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186416415 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1+6y+9z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}1+6y+9z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186416417 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416418 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416421 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416422 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416424 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416426 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416427 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-6y+9z \\\\ 4-y \\\\ 2-z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}1-6y+9z \\\\ 4-y \\\\ 2-z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186416429 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416435 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=-5 \\\\  & y=3 \\\\  & z=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=-5$, $y=3$, and $z=2.$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=-5 \\\\  & y=3 \\\\  & z=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the solution $x=-5$, $y=3$, and $z=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186416436 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416437 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -4 & -24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416438 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 24 & -36 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 24 & -36 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+6y-9z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-6y+9z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-6y+9z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 24 & -36 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 24 & -36 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & -18 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+6y-9z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-6y+9z \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-6y+9z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416481 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n- **II.** The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- **III.** Any $5\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n- **II.** The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ has one negative singular value.\n- **III.** Any $5\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values."] [:db/cas 17592186416482 :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]?$" "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186416482 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}17-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (17-\\lambda )(17-\\lambda )-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +289-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +225 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}17-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (17-\\lambda )(17-\\lambda )-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +289-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +225 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416483 :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186416483 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416484 :question/problem "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186416484 :question/worked-solution "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda -5\\lambda +40-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda -5\\lambda +40-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416485 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $4\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- **II.** The singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ are non-negative.\n- **III.** Any $3\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $4\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- **II.** The singular values of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ are non-negative.\n- **III.** Any $3\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular 