x}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416566 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416568 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416570 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416571 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416572 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416573 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416574 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416575 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416576 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416577 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416579 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416580 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416581 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416582 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416583 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416596 :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & =2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{2} & =4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{3} & =2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a & 2 \\\\ b & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & c & -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & =2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{2} & =4{c}_{1}-2{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{3} & =2{c}_{1}-3{c}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & a & 2 \\\\ b & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & c & -3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416596 :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=4,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2.$" "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,[{b}_{3}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=4,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186416597 :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" "Let $B={\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$"] [:db/cas 17592186416597 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416598 :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}$ and $C={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" "Let $B={\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}$ and $C={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$"] [:db/cas 17592186416598 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416599 :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & a & 7 \\\\ -1 & 4 & b \\\\ 4 & c & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & a & 7 \\\\ -1 & 4 & b \\\\ 4 & c & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416599 :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=8,$ $b=5,$ and $c=7.$" "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=8,$ $b=5,$ and $c=7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186416600 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ."] [:db/cas 17592186416600 :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416601 :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416601 :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{ and }\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{ and }\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416602 :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C$ given that\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C$ given that\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416602 :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416603 :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416603 :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416604 :question/problem "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $S$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" "Let $B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $S$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$"] [:db/cas 17592186416604 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416605 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ."] [:db/cas 17592186416605 :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416606 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ."] [:db/cas 17592186416606 :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-6)\\cdot 4-(-5)\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-6)\\cdot 4-(-5)\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416946 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186416946 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 32\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 9 & -8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 9 & -8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-8 \\\\ 4{x}_{2}=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}-4(5)=-8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+5=9.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =x \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives the system with the augmented matrix \n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 9 & -8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 9 & -8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ -12 & 16 & 32\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 20 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-8 \\\\ 4{x}_{2}=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}-4(5)=-8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+5=9.$"] [:db/cas 17592186416951 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416952 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416954 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416957 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416959 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416961 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416968 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416970 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416971 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416972 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416979 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices above, write the following expression as a Hadamard product. Here $(\\,\\cdot \\,)$ denotes matrix multiplication.\n\n| $(AB)\\cdot (By)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $⊙$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-6$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |  |  |" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices above, write the following expression as a Hadamard product. Here $(\\,\\cdot \\,)$ denotes matrix multiplication.\n\n| $(AB)\\cdot (By)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $⊙$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-6$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186416979 :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $u$ and $v$ can be expressed as the matrix product\n\n$$\nv⊙u=\\text{diag}(v)u.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBy=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AB)\\cdot (By) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\text{diag}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}])[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]⊙[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The Hadamard product of two compatible vectors $u$ and $v$ can be expressed as the matrix product\n\n$$\nv⊙u=\\text{diag}(v)u.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBy=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AB)\\cdot (By) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\text{diag}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix})\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416980 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nA⊙B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,A⊙C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfind $A⊙(B+C)+A⊙(B⊙C).$" "Given that\n\n$$\nA⊙B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,A⊙C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nfind $A⊙(B+C)+A⊙(B⊙C).$"] [:db/cas 17592186416980 :question/worked-solution "For compatible matrices $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ and scalar $\\alpha ,$ the Hadamard product has the following properties:\n\n- Commutativity:\n\n$$\nA⊙B=B⊙A\n$$\n\n- Associativity:\n\n$$\n(A⊙B)⊙C=A⊙(B⊙C)\n$$\n\n- Distributivity over Addition:\n\n$$\nA⊙(B+C)=A⊙B+A⊙C\n$$\n\n- Scalar Distributivity:\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A⊙B)=(\\alpha A)⊙B=A⊙(\\alpha B)\n$$\n\nIn this case, by the associativity and distributivity over addition of the Hadamard product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A⊙(B+C)+A⊙(B⊙C) & =A⊙B+A⊙C+(A⊙B)⊙C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]⊙[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+4 & 2+6 \\\\ 1+6 & 3+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For compatible matrices $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ and scalar $\\alpha ,$ the Hadamard product has the following properties:\n\n- Commutativity:\n\n$$\nA⊙B=B⊙A\n$$\n\n- Associativity:\n\n$$\n(A⊙B)⊙C=A⊙(B⊙C)\n$$\n\n- Distributivity over Addition:\n\n$$\nA⊙(B+C)=A⊙B+A⊙C\n$$\n\n- Scalar Distributivity:\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A⊙B)=(\\alpha A)⊙B=A⊙(\\alpha B)\n$$\n\nIn this case, by the associativity and distributivity over addition of the Hadamard product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A⊙(B+C)+A⊙(B⊙C) & =A⊙B+A⊙C+(A⊙B)⊙C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}⊙\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+4 & 2+6 \\\\ 1+6 & 3+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416981 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nA⊙B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,A⊙C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind $A⊙(B+C).$" "Given that\n\n$$\nA⊙B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,A⊙C=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nfind $A⊙(B+C).$"] [:db/cas 17592186416981 :question/worked-solution "For compatible matrices $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ and scalar $\\alpha ,$ the Hadamard product has the following properties:\n\n- Commutativity:\n\n$$\nA⊙B=B⊙A\n$$\n\n- Associativity:\n\n$$\n(A⊙B)⊙C=A⊙(B⊙C)\n$$\n\n- Distributivity over Addition:\n\n$$\nA⊙(B+C)=A⊙B+A⊙C\n$$\n\n- Scalar Distributivity:\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A⊙B)=(\\alpha A)⊙B=A⊙(\\alpha B)\n$$\n\nIn this case, by the distributivity over the addition of the Hadamard product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A⊙(B+C) & =A⊙B+A⊙C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1+5 & 4+(-3) \\\\ 3+2 & -2+6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For compatible matrices $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ and scalar $\\alpha ,$ the Hadamard product has the following properties:\n\n- Commutativity:\n\n$$\nA⊙B=B⊙A\n$$\n\n- Associativity:\n\n$$\n(A⊙B)⊙C=A⊙(B⊙C)\n$$\n\n- Distributivity over Addition:\n\n$$\nA⊙(B+C)=A⊙B+A⊙C\n$$\n\n- Scalar Distributivity:\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A⊙B)=(\\alpha A)⊙B=A⊙(\\alpha B)\n$$\n\nIn this case, by the distributivity over the addition of the Hadamard product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A⊙(B+C) & =A⊙B+A⊙C \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+5 & 4+(-3) \\\\ 3+2 & -2+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186416982 :question/problem "Given the matrices\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n in which of the following expressions is the Hadamard product well-defined?\n- **I.** $YX⊙Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$\n- **II.** $X⊙Yu$\n- **III.** $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)⊙YX{X}^{T}$\n\n*Hint: Here, $\\text{diag}(v)$ is a diagonal matrix with the entries of $v$ on the diagonal.*\n\nSelect all that apply." "Given the matrices\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\,Y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,u=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n in which of the following expressions is the Hadamard product well-defined?\n- **I.** $YX⊙Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$\n- **II.** $X⊙Yu$\n- **III.** $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)⊙YX{X}^{T}$\n\n*Hint: Here, $\\text{diag}(v)$ is a diagonal matrix with the entries of $v$ on the diagonal.*\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186416989 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416990 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416991 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416992 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416993 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416994 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416995 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186416996 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\e