3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417011 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417012 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417013 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417019 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417019 :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda =9,$ and\n\n$$\nJ=A=9I=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda =9,$ and\n\n$$\nJ=A=9I=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417020 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417020 :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 1 & 9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda )-(-1)\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda +64 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -8)(\\lambda -8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=8.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(8)=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 1 & 9-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda )-(-1)\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda +64 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -8)(\\lambda -8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=8.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(8)=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417021 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417021 :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-10-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 8 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-10-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-(-2)\\cdot 8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)(\\lambda +6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-6.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-6)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-10-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 8 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-10-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-(-2)\\cdot 8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)(\\lambda +6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-6.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-6)=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417022 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417022 :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ and\n\n$$\nJ=A=-I=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1,$ and\n\n$$\nJ=A=-I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417072 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2+z \\\\ -z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ -z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186417073 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186417075 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2+z \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186417076 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2+z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186417077 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2+z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186417081 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-z=2 \\\\  & y+2z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+z \\\\ y=-2z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the latter can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-z=2 \\\\  & y+2z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+z \\\\ y=-2z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the latter can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417082 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2-3k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=4 \\\\  & (2-3k)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{2}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{2}{3}.$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2-3k & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=4 \\\\  & (2-3k)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{2}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ 