186417168 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417169 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417170 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417171 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417172 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417173 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186417184 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,[x]_{S}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417184 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417185 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417185 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417186 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417186 :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-22 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417187 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417187 :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-37 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-37 \\\\ 28\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417188 :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}},\\,C={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417188 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417189 :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" "$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}},\\,C={\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}},\\,[x]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417189 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -9 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -9 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186417190 :question/problem "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3}.$" "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186417190 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that since $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] 