 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & ={{field-12}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & {5}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true for all {{field-15}}."] [:db/cas 17592186418038 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{1} & {5}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$ \n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k} & {5}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k+1} & {5}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1}={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k+1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{k}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k} & {5}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5({5}^{k}) & 4({5}^{k})+({5}^{k}-1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k}\\cdot {5}^{1} & {5}^{k}\\cdot {5}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{k+1} & {5}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nis true for all $n\\ge 1.$" "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{1} & {5}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$ \n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{k} & {5}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{k+1} & {5}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{k} & {5}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5({5}^{k}) & 4({5}^{k})+({5}^{k}-1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{k}\\cdot {5}^{1} & {5}^{k}\\cdot {5}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{k+1} & {5}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n${\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nis true for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186418092 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186418093 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186418094 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt