nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186422154 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\n Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\n Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\n Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\n Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186422155 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-3x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f20c6478af528b45ab2ea7dfded3e835c49a79178e11d7dd06bb13760874bbdb.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-3x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f20c6478af528b45ab2ea7dfded3e835c49a79178e11d7dd06bb13760874bbdb.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186422156 :question/problem "![](images/45/450d43a4cbf6dad8d54edbb66fc4a6e44e491290e1dc5d1303057fb0eb7928a7.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$>0.$" "![](images/45/450d43a4cbf6dad8d54edbb66fc4a6e44e491290e1dc5d1303057fb0eb7928a7.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186422156 :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2dace7c4f02fa0575d70264f33805d15470fa0894622c4d26d80fc1221aa2c54.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40f0bf749448807b1a3aa2b593ae2e30acf26fabedbdc45da75e338e84c328f2.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{2}$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{1}>0.$" "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2dace7c4f02fa0575d70264f33805d15470fa0894622c4d26d80fc1221aa2c54.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40f0bf749448807b1a3aa2b593ae2e30acf26fabedbdc45da75e338e84c328f2.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{2}$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{1}>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186422466 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y+2z+2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}y+2z+2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186422467 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186422469 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2-y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}2-y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186422470 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y-2z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}y-2z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186422471 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186422472 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186422473 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186422474 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\begin{bmatrix}y-2z-1 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186422475 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186422478 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z=-2 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=y-2z-2 \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}y-2z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+2z=-2 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final two equations, we can remove these equations from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$, and we have two free variables, $y,$ and $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y$ and $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=y-2z-2 \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ and $z$ (the free variables) are arbitrary numbers.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}y-2z-2 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186422479 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -11 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -6 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -7 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423032 :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2b74f78040024982099dac9b8670ef0f3fddd8bd5c88c3fa2b097659876c15a.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "![](images/f2/f2b74f78040024982099dac9b8670ef0f3fddd8bd5c88c3fa2b097659876c15a.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423032 :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{i,j\\}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4e/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdb71769b9035f137125e4242f9653ba9d346a4507042a8b864227e23498b781.png)" "Consider the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{i,j\\}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4e/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdb71769b9035f137125e4242f9653ba9d346a4507042a8b864227e23498b781.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186423125 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423126 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423127 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423128 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423129 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423131 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]?$" "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186423131 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]=3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}=3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."] [:db/cas 17592186423134 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1 & 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1 & 2-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423141 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 2-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423142 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1 & 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1 & 4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423146 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423148 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423160 :question/problem "Given the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i} & 3-8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$, find the result of applying to $A$ the following elementary row operation:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4\\text{i}}{1-\\text{i}}){R}_{1}\n$$" "Given the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i} & 3-8\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$, find the result of applying to $A$ the following elementary row operation:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4\\text{i}}{1-\\text{i}}){R}_{1}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423160 :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the row operations to $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i} & 3-8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4\\text{i}}{1-\\text{i}}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i}+(-\\frac{4\\text{i}(1-\\text{i})}{1-\\text{i}}) & 3-8\\text{i}+(-\\frac{4\\text{i}(-1+3\\text{i})}{1-\\text{i}})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i}-4\\text{i} & \\frac{(3-8\\text{i})(1-\\text{i})+12+4\\text{i}}{1-\\text{i}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i}-4\\text{i} & \\frac{7-7\\text{i}}{1-\\text{i}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the result  is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Let's apply the row operations to $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i} & 3-8\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4\\text{i}}{1-\\text{i}}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i}+(-\\frac{4\\text{i}(1-\\text{i})}{1-\\text{i}}) & 3-8\\text{i}+(-\\frac{4\\text{i}(-1+3\\text{i})}{1-\\text{i}})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i}-4\\text{i} & \\frac{(3-8\\text{i})(1-\\text{i})+12+4\\text{i}}{1-\\text{i}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4\\text{i}-4\\text{i} & \\frac{7-7\\text{i}}{1-\\text{i}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the result  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} & -1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423171 :answer/value "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=1" "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=1"] [:db/cas 17592186423172 :answer/value "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0" "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0"] [:db/cas 17592186423173 :answer/value "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-1" "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=-1"] [:db/cas 17592186423174 :answer/value "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=1" "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=1"] [:db/cas 17592186423175 :answer/value "r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423177 :question/problem "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(0,1,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $P(0,1,1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423177 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the position vector of $P$ is $p=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $n=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that the position vector of $P$ is $p=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $n=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423258 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423259 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423260 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423261 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423262 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423263 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423264 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423265 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423266 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423267 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423270 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the following matrix: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Find the inverse of the following matrix: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423270 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given matrix is diagonal.\n\nThe inverse of a diagonal matrix can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{a}_{11}} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{{a}_{22}} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & \\frac{1}{{a}_{nn}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Notice that the given matrix is diagonal.\n\nThe inverse of a diagonal matrix can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{{a}_{11}} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{{a}_{22}} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & \\frac{1}{{a}_{nn}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423271 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the following matrix: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Find the inverse of the following matrix: \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423271 :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given matrix is diagonal.\n\nThe inverse of a diagonal matrix can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{a}_{11}} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{{a}_{22}} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & \\frac{1}{{a}_{nn}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Notice that the given matrix is diagonal.\n\nThe inverse of a diagonal matrix can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{{a}_{11}} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{{a}_{22}} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & \\frac{1}{{a}_{nn}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}={\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423355 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & b & 0 \\\\ 0 & c & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & b & 0 \\\\ 0 & c & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186423355 :question/worked-solution "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+(-2){x}_{2}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{2}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{3}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+1{x}_{3}{y}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=1.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-2)+1=0.\n$$" "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+(-2){x}_{2}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{2}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{3}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+1{x}_{3}{y}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=1.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-2)+1=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423356 :question/problem "The linear form $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=ax,$ where $a=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].$  Evaluate the function when $x=[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ \\,\\,1 \\\\ \\,2\\end{aligned}].$" "The linear form $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=ax,$ where $a=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$  Evaluate the function when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ \\,\\,1 \\\\ \\,2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423356 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ \\,\\,1 \\\\ \\,2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ \\,\\,1 \\\\ \\,2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10-8-3+4 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ \\,\\,1 \\\\ \\,2\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ \\,\\,1 \\\\ \\,2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10-8-3+4 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423357 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186423357 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =(-5){x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-2){x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-5{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(-5{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =-5{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =(-5){x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+(-2){x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-5{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(-5{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-2{y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =-5{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423358 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}^{T}\\,Ay$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the bilinear form $B(x,y)={x}^{T}\\,Ay$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423358 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ay \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ay \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423359 :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & b & -2 \\\\ c & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{3}-2{x}_{3}{y}_{1}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b-c?$" "$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & b & -2 \\\\ c & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the bilinear form $B(x,y)=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{3}-2{x}_{3}{y}_{1}$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b-c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186423359 :question/worked-solution "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{3}-2{x}_{3}{y}_{1} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{1}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+(-5){x}_{2}{y}_{2}+(-2){x}_{2}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,(-2){x}_{3}{y}_{1}+0{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{3}{y}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-2.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=3+(-5)-(-2)=0.\n$$" "Each term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form corresponds to the entry ${a}_{ij}$ of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}-5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{y}_{3}-2{x}_{3}{y}_{1} \\\\  & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{1}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,0{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+(-5){x}_{2}{y}_{2}+(-2){x}_{2}{y}_{3} \\\\  & +\\,(-2){x}_{3}{y}_{1}+0{x}_{3}{y}_{2}+0{x}_{3}{y}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding matrix of the bilinear form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-2.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b-c=3+(-5)-(-2)=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423360 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding bilinear form ${x}^{T}\\,Ay$ in terms of the components of $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186423360 :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{y}_{j}$ in the expression of the bilinear form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+1{x}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a bilinear form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ay & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})+{x}_{2}(3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423361 :question/problem "Consider the bilinear form $B(x,y),$ given by \n\n$$\nB(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate $B(x,y)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" "Consider the bilinear form $B(x,y),$ given by \n\n$$\nB(x,y)={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate $B(x,y)$ when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423361 :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSubstituting these into the bilinear expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}. \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0-0+0-1-2 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We are given that \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSubstituting these into the bilinear expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}. \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0-0+0-1-2 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423362 :question/problem "The linear form $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=ax,$ where $a=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$  Evaluate the function when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$" "The linear form $f(x)$ is defined as $f(x)=ax,$ where $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$  Evaluate the function when $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423362 :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 5+4\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =4+15-12 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =ax \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 5+4\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =4+15-12 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423503 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423511 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423526 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423537 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423540 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423546 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423549 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423567 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423570 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423571 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186423587 :question/problem "Given the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ \\sqrt{74}\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖2v‖.$" "Given the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ \\sqrt{74}\\end{bmatrix}$, find $‖2v‖.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423588 :question/problem "Given $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖a‖.$" "Given $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$, find $‖a‖.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423589 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $0.$" "Find the distance between the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -18 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423590 :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ k \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find $k$, if the distance between the vectors is $\\text{d}(x,y)=\\sqrt{83}.$" "Let $x=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ k \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $k$, if the distance between the vectors is $\\text{d}(x,y)=\\sqrt{83}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423591 :question/problem "Normalize the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" "Normalize the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423591 :question/worked-solution "To normalize a vector $x,$ we have to divide the vector by its norm: $\\frac{x}{‖x‖}.$ Therefore, to normalize $x,$ we first calculate $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+{x}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+16+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $x$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{‖x‖} & =\\frac{x}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{-1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To normalize a vector $x,$ we have to divide the vector by its norm: $\\frac{x}{‖x‖}.$ Therefore, to normalize $x,$ we first calculate $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+{x}_{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+16+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $x$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{‖x‖} & =\\frac{x}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{-1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423592 :question/problem "Given the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖4v‖.$" "Given the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$, find $‖4v‖.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423593 :question/problem "Find $k$, if $u=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{k}{4}\\end{aligned}]$ is a unit vector." "Find $k$, if $u=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{k}{4}\\end{bmatrix}$ is a unit vector."] [:db/cas 17592186423594 :question/problem "Given $w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖w‖.$" "Given $w=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{10}\\end{bmatrix}$, find $‖w‖.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423595 :question/problem "Given $x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖x‖.$" "Given $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$, find $‖x‖.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423596 :question/problem "Normalize the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" "Normalize the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423596 :question/worked-solution "To normalize a vector $x,$ we have to divide the vector by its norm: $\\frac{x}{‖x‖}.$ Therefore, to normalize $x,$ we first calculate $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $x$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{‖x‖} & =\\frac{x}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To normalize a vector $x,$ we have to divide the vector by its norm: $\\frac{x}{‖x‖}.$ Therefore, to normalize $x,$ we first calculate $‖x‖$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $x$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{‖x‖} & =\\frac{x}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186423597 :question/problem "Given the vectors $a=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$, find the distance $\\text{d}(a,b).$" "Given the vectors $a=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$, find the distance $\\text{d}(a,b).$"] [:db/cas 17592186423598 :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2k \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ Find $k$, if $‖x‖=3\\sqrt{6}.$" "Let $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2k \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $k$, if $‖x‖=3\\sqrt{6}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423599 :question/problem "Given $e=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$, find $‖e‖.$" "Given $e=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$, find $‖e‖.$"] [:db/cas 17592186423601 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186423602 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186423603 :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}" "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}"] [:db/cas 17592186423607 :answer/value "B=\